Z||000005||Tigger||11-02-2000||09:36 AM||||I seen something about money management skills on Oprah one day.<P>One tip I remember they gave from the show was that we should keep the money in our wallets organized.  For example put all $1 bills together, then all $5 bills, all $10 bills, and so on.  I don't know if that's supposed to help, I'm not kidding that was on the show.||||reg||3
Z||000006||mrfixit58||11-02-2000||10:09 AM||||One: Throw all your credit cards in a box and put a lock on it.<P>Two: Put yourself on a REALISTIC budget.<P>Three: If you usually buy your lunch, start making a sandwich or taking leftovers (I started saving between $25- $35 a week doing this).<P>Four: Use your new found wealth to pay off your credit card debt. If you have several cards, take advantage of a low interest rate card and consolitate all the debt on that ONE card.<P>Five: If your company offers a retirement investment program. Start participating. Make it a goal to work your way up to the maximum matching percentage (i.e., if the match up to 6%, your throwing money away if your not participating). <P>Five b: If your company doesn't offer a savings program, cunsult with a broker and start you own fund. Start small (maybe $50 a month) but keep faithfull (e.g., $50 per month @ 8% per year growth is $29,650 in 20 years and $70,000 in 30 years).<P>Six: The earlier you start saving, the better. Time really goes by quickly....<P>Good Luck,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||onfire||11-02-2000||01:54 PM||||You could always get a second job. That way you have no free time to spend money, and you get a second paycheck.<P>------------------<BR>I'm not an idiot. I just play one on my p.c.||||reg||1
Z||000008||bHooper||11-02-2000||03:59 PM||||Scott, it sounds like we are in a similar position.  However, I have identified my culprit.  No, it's not the girlfriend... she actually comes from a long line of penny pincher/cheap skates.<P>It's... it's the Fiero!  I have dumped almost a $G in the suspension this week, and still won't have new brakes or kybs or koni's!<P>My suggestion, sell your Fiero (scary thought!) and follow the teachings of Jack Freeman, success guru:http://www.jackfreeman.net/<P><BR>(jk about the fiero)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||Old Lar||11-02-2000||07:06 PM||||The periodic savings is a must, start young and don't touch the money.  IRAs are great if you don't have or qualify for a company sponsored 401 plan.  Don't touch the money once invested.  Let me repeat don't touch the money.  Pay yourself first.  The IRA/401 should be invested in a diversified portfolio.  Blue chip stocks, small companies, foreign stocks, especially when you are young &lt;40. Lots of financial planners out there and stuff on the internet as far as investment diversity.<BR>Get rid of the credit cards if you carry a balance. Those rates should be made illegal.  10-20% interest.<P>I am not against credit cards, I use them every day, but I keep an Idea of what I charge and pay it off.  I use a GM Master Card or Discover.  The GM card %5 toward a new GM card up to $500/year for 7 years.  Thats up to $3500 off your next new GM card.  That is charging $10000/year.  If you don't want to buy a GM car, Discover pays a paltry 1% on your charges. But they pay you. $10000 charged something &lt; $100 back. When I bought my home computer and all the periferals,($4000) I had the money in the bank,  I charge the purchased on Discover and paid it off the next billing cycle.  Discover paid me $40 for the purchase. Do not...DO NOT carry a balance on Discover 16-20% interest.<BR>You need a "rainy day" reserve fund for emergencies.<BR>My policy is that I don't pay more in interest  than I can make on investments.  The 90's was a great time for investing.  Thats why you don't touch the money.  It grew like crazy.  The 80's were so-so buying in when the investments were flat.||||reg||4
Z||000010||StuGood||11-02-2000||11:11 PM||||Old Lar's got it right! 1st: Pay off <B>all</B> your credit cards.  2nd: Do the automatic investing thing whether it's a 401-k (super!), or just depositing it in a money market or savings account before you give yourself a chance to spend it.  <P>Credit cards are a real convenience, but don't use more than one, and whatever you do, <B>never</B> carry a balance!  Pay off the entire balance <B>every</B> time the bill comes.<P><BR>Look at it this way: If you pay it off right away, they're lending you some money for thirty days - for free  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">!  Carry a balance, and you're giving <B>them</B> 18% (or whatever the current outrageous rate is) on the money  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">.  You decide.  Not too tough, is it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ?<P>Once, a friend of mine gave me her credit card, and told me to hide it away somewhere and not give it back until she paid it all off.  Many months later, she showed me her statement - "No payment due," the balance was zero!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> The message is - if spending's a real problem, you can get help from someone you trust.        <P>I use Discover all the time (since 1987), but never pay them a nickel of interest, because I <B>never</B> carry a balance.  Plus, as Old Lar mentioned, they actually <B>pay</B> you for using it.  Do I ever use it, and buy something, so I'll get more money back?  Heck, no. I just buy stuff I would've gotten with cash anyway.  And pay it off as soon as the bill comes.<P>I suppose I'd be using the GM card, but they haven't made any cars that interest me since, oh, about 1988...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Anyway, sorry I got so windy, and Good Luck <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||14
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||11-03-2000||12:12 AM||||All very good info... I've had a visa card for 2 years now, and I always try to pay the full amount.. in fact I always did that until I started school... now I have a $700+ balance from buying textbooks, food, etc. and I haven't been able to pay it all off yet because I have no time to work.. so I'm paying it off a couple hundred at a time, which really bugs me..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Oh well, as long as I still make more than the minimum payment, my credit is ok.. but I really hate not paying it all off.<P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>   <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 11-03-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000012||Philphine||11-03-2000||02:54 AM||||about the best thing i do is max out my 401k at work. another thing some one in the national guard told me is try to save all my guard money and each time it builds up enough buy a cd. keep buying them each time you save enough (say a $500 cd would take about 3 months to save up for with some left over). let them roll over and eventually you have cds constantly coming to maturity.<P>of course i spend my guard pay (along with al my other money) on my cars.<P>actually, i would say if a person has more than 2 fieros, they know nothing about money management.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe (cheap thrill)||||reg||1
Z||000013||zeusrekning||11-03-2000||06:21 AM||||Try reading "the richest man in babylon".<BR>The book is supposed to be non fiction. well just read it. but the philosopy is to pay yourself %10 of your income no matter what.Then pay your bills and spend what ever. Just don't spend the 10%. The saved money are your "children". You must teach them to work for you. By investing properly.<BR>Just remember not to trust the sheepsman to buy you fine diamonds. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> it really is a nice book.   I'm still trying to save quarters, so i guess it really dosen't help to much. ||||reg||1
